HST Office: safe user interface language selection.

- A stored UI language written in another form of an installed language (en, en-GB,
  EN-us, vi-VN, vi_VN; hand-edited or set by an administrator) resolves to that
  installed language (svtools langhelp) instead of being treated as "not installed".
- Startup no longer erases a selected language that is not (or no longer) installed:
  the session falls back like "Default" and the choice applies again once the
  language pack is back. A registered language whose translations (vcl.mo) are
  missing uses the built-in English UI for the session instead of a half-translated
  one (desktop langselect).
- Tools > Options > Languages and Locales > General: the user interface list shows
  the language such a value resolves to, writes only when the user picks another
  entry, is disabled when an administrator finalized UILocale, and reports a failed
  write instead of offering a restart that would change nothing (cui optgdlg,
  RID_CUISTR_UI_LANGUAGE_NOT_SAVED + Vietnamese translation).
- Restart after a UI language change keeps every open document: the restart dialog
  dispatches the new AutoRecovery job vnd.sun.star.autorecovery:/doSessionRestart
  (also used by the HST > Ngôn ngữ giao diện menu). It stores every document to its
  recovery file (never to the original, even with "always save the original") and
  checks that each changed document has a current, complete, non-empty one, that no
  changed document is untracked or password-protected; only then are the documents
  closed (no modified flag is reset, unlike doSessionQuietQuit), the session is
  marked for restore and the restart requested. On the next start the session
  restore reopens them, changed ones still modified. Otherwise nothing is closed and
  the ordinary restart (with save prompts) runs; likewise without AutoRecovery
  (--norestore) or while another AutoRecovery job runs.
- The primary-language fallback never crosses scripts (zh, sr-Latn, ...), and the
  translations check skips tags whose folder name it does not derive (sr@latin).

